From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 22:01:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: lg-sw43402: send display-on after the first frame,
 like the vendor

On a DSC cmd-mode panel the DDIC's GRAM holds garbage after a re-init, so
display-on (0x29) sent before the first frame has landed flashes that garbage.
Reported from the device 2026-09-20 as full-screen RGB noise, one frame long,
on every unblank, and worst when the power key is pressed repeatedly.

This was already known and worked around with a fixed 80 ms delay. That is a
race, not a fix: too short and the flash returns whenever the first frame is
late -- which hammering the power key makes likely, and is exactly how it was
reported -- too long and every wake is needlessly black. Raising the knob to
400 ms removed the flash on hardware, which is what proved the mechanism. It
is also not shippable: nobody wants 400 ms of black on every wake.

The vendor does not use a delay at all. mdss_mdp_cmd_kickoff() turns the panel
on INSIDE the kickoff and fires MDSS_EVENT_POST_PANEL_ON after
__mdss_mdp_kickoff() returns, with its own comment saying why: "the panel is
turned on only when we send the first frame and not during cmd start. This is
to ensure that no artifacts are seen on the panel." Their
qcom,mdss-dsi-post-panel-on-command is `05 01 00 00 00 00 01 29` -- wait field
zero. It is ordering, not timing.

dsi_host already counts completed cmd-mode frames for the DCS/frame interlock,
so the same ordering is expressible directly: msm_dsi_wait_one_frame() blocks
on frame_wait until frame_done passes the value the panel snapshotted in
enable(). Display-on now lands one frame after enable -- about 17 ms -- and
never before the GRAM has been written.

The knob survives as a fallback CEILING rather than a delay. If no frame ever
arrives the panel still sends 0x29, because without it the panel never scans
at all (no TE, "frame done timeout") and a dark panel is worse than one flash;
it warns when that happens, so a real regression in the frame path cannot hide
behind a silent fallback. 0 still means "send inline", kept for bisecting.

The two helpers are EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL and declared in the panel rather than
included: msm_drv.h is private to the msm driver and this panel is a separate
module. The coupling is narrow and stated at the declaration -- if either
signature moves it breaks at link time, not silently.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c       | 54 ++++++++++++++++
 drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_drv.h            | 10 +++
 drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c | 80 ++++++++++++++++++++----
 3 files changed, 131 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
index c899a76766b3..7558572051d4 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c
@@ -1311,6 +1311,60 @@ static bool dsi_frame_pending(struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host)
 		     READ_ONCE(msm_host->frame_done)) > 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * How many cmd-mode frames this panel's host has COMPLETED (CMD_MDP_DONE),
+ * and a bounded wait for the next one.
+ *
+ * WHY A PANEL WANTS THIS. On a DSC cmd-mode panel the DDIC's GRAM holds
+ * garbage after a re-init, so sending display-on (0x29) before the first
+ * frame has landed flashes that garbage on every unblank -- full-screen RGB
+ * noise, reported from the device 2026-09-20. The panel driver worked around
+ * it with a fixed delay, which is a race: it is either too short (the flash
+ * comes back when the first frame is late, which hammering the power key
+ * makes likely) or too long, and 400 ms of black on every wake is not what
+ * anyone wants.
+ *
+ * The vendor does not use a delay at all. In mdss_mdp_cmd_kickoff() it turns
+ * the panel on inside the kickoff and then fires MDSS_EVENT_POST_PANEL_ON
+ * AFTER __mdss_mdp_kickoff() returns, with its own comment saying why: "the
+ * panel is turned on only when we send the first frame ... to ensure that no
+ * artifacts are seen on the panel". Their post-panel-on command carries a
+ * zero wait field. It is ordering, not timing.
+ *
+ * This is the same thing expressed against the counter this host already
+ * keeps. Returns false on timeout WITHOUT having waited for a frame, so the
+ * caller can decide between "send anyway" and "give up"; a panel that never
+ * receives a frame must still be able to come up.
+ */
+bool msm_dsi_wait_one_frame(u32 since, unsigned int timeout_ms)
+{
+	struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host = READ_ONCE(cmd_sync_host);
+	long left;
+
+	/*
+	 * No host registered for cmd sync means nothing is counting frames,
+	 * so there is nothing to wait for and reporting "waited" would be a
+	 * lie. brain/laws/empty-must-mean-unknown-never-changed.
+	 */
+	if (!msm_host)
+		return false;
+
+	left = wait_event_timeout(msm_host->frame_wait,
+				  (s32)(READ_ONCE(msm_host->frame_done) -
+					since) > 0,
+				  msecs_to_jiffies(timeout_ms));
+	return left > 0;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(msm_dsi_wait_one_frame);
+
+u32 msm_dsi_frames_done(void)
+{
+	struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host = READ_ONCE(cmd_sync_host);
+
+	return msm_host ? READ_ONCE(msm_host->frame_done) : 0;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(msm_dsi_frames_done);
+
 void msm_dsi_cmd_kickoff_begin(void)
 {
 	struct msm_dsi_host *msm_host = READ_ONCE(cmd_sync_host);
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_drv.h b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_drv.h
index 74b7b3239aee..f1e931327c5c 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_drv.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_drv.h
@@ -306,6 +306,8 @@ bool msm_dsi_wide_bus_enabled(struct msm_dsi *msm_dsi);
 struct drm_dsc_config *msm_dsi_get_dsc_config(struct msm_dsi *msm_dsi);
 const char *msm_dsi_get_te_source(struct msm_dsi *msm_dsi);
 void msm_dsi_cmd_kickoff_begin(void);
+bool msm_dsi_wait_one_frame(u32 since, unsigned int timeout_ms);
+u32 msm_dsi_frames_done(void);
 #else
 static inline void __init msm_dsi_register(void)
 {
@@ -351,6 +353,14 @@ static inline const char *msm_dsi_get_te_source(struct msm_dsi *msm_dsi)
 static inline void msm_dsi_cmd_kickoff_begin(void)
 {
 }
+static inline bool msm_dsi_wait_one_frame(u32 since, unsigned int timeout_ms)
+{
+	return false;
+}
+static inline u32 msm_dsi_frames_done(void)
+{
+	return 0;
+}
 #endif
 
 struct msm_dp;
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c
index f1e3c7e5db78..45984e284369 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-lg-sw43402.c
@@ -16,6 +16,18 @@
 #include <drm/drm_modes.h>
 #include <drm/drm_panel.h>
 #include <drm/drm_print.h>
+
+/*
+ * From drivers/gpu/drm/msm/dsi/dsi_host.c, declared here rather than included
+ * because msm_drv.h is private to the msm driver and this panel is a separate
+ * module. The coupling is deliberate and narrow: this panel needs to know when
+ * the FIRST FRAME has reached the DDIC's GRAM before it may send display-on,
+ * and the msm DSI host is the only thing that counts completed cmd-mode
+ * frames. See display_on_timeout_ms. Both are EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL there; if
+ * either signature moves, this breaks at link time rather than silently.
+ */
+bool msm_dsi_wait_one_frame(u32 since, unsigned int timeout_ms);
+u32 msm_dsi_frames_done(void);
 #include <drm/display/drm_dsc.h>
 #include <drm/display/drm_dsc_helper.h>
 
@@ -41,16 +53,33 @@
 #define LG_SW43402_BRIGHTNESS	(LG_SW43402_BRIGHTNESS_MAX * 40 / 100)
 
 /*
- * Delay between enable() and the display-on (0x29) DCS. After a re-init the
- * DDIC's GRAM holds garbage, and enable() runs before the first frame is
- * written -- sending 0x29 inline flashes that garbage on every unblank
- * (RGB-noise bands under the incoming image). The vendor ships 0x29 as
- * qcom,mdss-dsi-POST-panel-on-command for the same reason: it goes out after
- * the first kickoff. ~5 frames of delay lets the first real frames land in
- * GRAM while the panel is still dark. 0 = send inline (old behavior).
+ * Display-on (0x29) waits for the FIRST COMPLETED FRAME, not for a duration.
+ *
+ * After a re-init the DDIC's GRAM holds garbage, and enable() runs before the
+ * first frame is written -- sending 0x29 inline flashes that garbage on every
+ * unblank as full-screen RGB noise. This was a fixed 80 ms delay, which is a
+ * race rather than a fix: too short and the flash returns whenever the first
+ * frame is late (hammering the power key makes that likely, and that is
+ * exactly how it was reported on 2026-09-20), too long and every wake is
+ * black for no reason. Raising it to 400 ms did remove the flash, which is
+ * what proved the mechanism -- and 400 ms of black on every unblank is not
+ * shippable.
+ *
+ * The vendor does not use a delay. mdss_mdp_cmd_kickoff() turns the panel on
+ * inside the kickoff and fires MDSS_EVENT_POST_PANEL_ON after
+ * __mdss_mdp_kickoff() returns; their post-panel-on command carries a ZERO
+ * wait field. It is ordering, not timing. msm_dsi_wait_one_frame() expresses
+ * the same ordering against the frame counter dsi_host already keeps, so
+ * display-on lands one frame after enable() -- about 17 ms -- and never
+ * before the GRAM has been written.
+ *
+ * This value is only the FALLBACK ceiling for that wait: if no frame ever
+ * arrives we still send 0x29, because without it the panel never scans at all
+ * (no TE, "frame done timeout") and a dark panel is worse than a brief flash.
+ * 0 = send inline, the old pre-2026-07 behaviour, kept for bisecting.
  */
-static uint display_on_delay_ms = 80;
-module_param(display_on_delay_ms, uint, 0644);
+static uint display_on_timeout_ms = 500;
+module_param(display_on_timeout_ms, uint, 0644);
 
 /*
  * vddio (1.8V I/O) and vpnl (panel VCC) are both wired to the panel node in
@@ -80,6 +109,8 @@ struct lg_sw43402 {
 	struct delayed_work bl_work;
 	bool prepared;
 	bool enabled;
+	/* frame counter snapshot taken in enable(); see display_on_timeout_ms */
+	u32 frames_at_enable;
 };
 
 static inline struct lg_sw43402 *to_lg_sw43402(struct drm_panel *panel)
@@ -224,9 +255,14 @@ static int lg_panel_enable(struct drm_panel *panel)
 	 * See display_on_delay_ms. The vsync source here is the WD timer, not
 	 * panel TE, so frames flow even while the display is still off.
 	 */
-	if (display_on_delay_ms) {
-		schedule_delayed_work(&ctx->display_on_work,
-				      msecs_to_jiffies(display_on_delay_ms));
+	if (display_on_timeout_ms) {
+		/*
+		 * Snapshot BEFORE the work is queued: the wait is for a frame
+		 * completed after this point, and a frame that landed earlier
+		 * wrote the previous image, not this one.
+		 */
+		ctx->frames_at_enable = msm_dsi_frames_done();
+		schedule_delayed_work(&ctx->display_on_work, 0);
 	} else {
 		ret = mipi_dsi_dcs_set_display_on(ctx->dsi);
 		if (ret < 0) {
@@ -258,7 +294,25 @@ static void lg_sw43402_display_on_work(struct work_struct *work)
 					      display_on_work.work);
 	int ret;
 
-	/* The panel may have been disabled again before the delay expired. */
+	/* The panel may have been disabled again before the frame arrived. */
+	if (!ctx->enabled)
+		return;
+
+	/*
+	 * Not a sleep. This returns as soon as one frame has COMPLETED, which
+	 * is what makes the unblank feel instant instead of waiting out a
+	 * worst-case delay. False means the timeout won: send 0x29 anyway,
+	 * because a panel that never scans is worse than one brief flash, and
+	 * say so -- a silent fallback here would hide a real regression in the
+	 * frame path.
+	 */
+	if (!msm_dsi_wait_one_frame(ctx->frames_at_enable,
+				    display_on_timeout_ms))
+		dev_warn(&ctx->dsi->dev,
+			 "no frame within %u ms of enable; display-on sent anyway (expect a flash)\n",
+			 display_on_timeout_ms);
+
+	/* Re-check: the wait can outlive a disable. */
 	if (!ctx->enabled)
 		return;
 
